WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.487
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.782 --> 00:00:09.240
WESLEY CHUN:
Greetings, developers,

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:10.770
and welcome to
Serverless Migration

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:14.100
Station, a miniseries that's
part of Serverless Expeditions.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.860
I'm Wesley, your conductor
on these short trips.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:18.555
Joining me is
Martin, who will help

00:00:18.555 --> 00:00:20.880
me help you navigate
the world of modernizing

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:23.490
your applications, and
Porter, our little friend here

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:26.793
who will help you get
from point A to point B.

00:00:26.793 --> 00:00:27.960
MARTIN OMANDER: Thanks, Wes.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:29.490
I'm happy to be here.

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:32.220
What are these migrations,
and why should developers

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:33.163
be interested?

00:00:33.163 --> 00:00:34.830
WESLEY CHUN: I'm glad
you asked, Martin.

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:37.260
Well, our job is to help
developers modernize

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:40.080
their apps, to take advantage
of new features and product

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.420
upgrades, to move to
a more modern release

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:44.310
of their development
language, and generally

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.530
keep their apps running
well to support theirs

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:48.630
or their customers' needs.

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:50.310
In each episode,
our friend Porter

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:52.920
here takes us on a short
excursion to the next Migration

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:53.730
Station.

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:56.130
Each ride attempts to
take the mystery out

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:58.230
of common migrations
that developers undergo

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:00.891
when modernizing their apps.

00:01:00.891 --> 00:01:02.300
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah, I get it.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:04.790
We're helping developers
take advantage of the latest

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:06.740
innovations from Google Cloud.

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:09.543
So which migrations are
we focusing on first, Wes?

00:01:09.543 --> 00:01:11.210
WESLEY CHUN: I'm glad
you asked, Martin.

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:13.280
The first set of migrations
we're going to tackle

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:15.800
is for original Python
App Engine users.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:19.070
Since its launch in 2008
as our first Cloud product,

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:21.410
we've continued to innovate
and open the platform,

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:24.980
as well as introduce
some new products.

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:27.530
MARTIN OMANDER: I'm familiar
with Cloud Functions and Cloud

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:28.250
Run.

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:30.320
Are you saying users
can run their App Engine

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.300
code on those platforms, too?

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:33.410
WESLEY CHUN: Yes, Martin.

00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:35.820
App Engine users have options.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:38.270
You can do nothing at all
and stay on App Engine,

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:40.880
you can modernize to the
next-generation App Engine

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:43.100
service, you can
break up larger apps

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:45.470
into micro-services
for Cloud Functions,

00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:48.530
or you can containerize
your app for Cloud Run.

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:51.470
Using Docker is another
way to stay on Python 2

00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:54.080
forever, for all of
you hard-core folks.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:56.510
If you don't want to learn
about Docker or containers,

00:01:56.510 --> 00:01:58.790
you can still containerize
using Buildpacks,

00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:01.010
but only Python 3 is supported.

00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:02.780
This series will
cover most of these,

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:06.817
but starts with helping you
move to unbundle services.

00:02:06.817 --> 00:02:07.650
MARTIN OMANDER: Hmm.

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:11.100
I like that Google Cloud is
embracing openness and helping

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:13.110
make my apps more portable.

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:16.770
But what's wrong with having
the legacy App Engine services,

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:17.330
anyway?

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:19.080
WESLEY CHUN: Well,
nothing at all, Martin.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:21.540
But while bundled
services are convenient,

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:24.430
App Engine isn't as
open as it can be.

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:27.810
For example, while webapp2 is
a great lightweight framework,

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:29.310
moving to something
more general,

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:32.070
like Flask, allows your
app to be more portable,

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:34.530
meaning you can run your
apps outside of App Engine

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:35.640
if desired.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:37.560
By the way, if you
don't use Flask,

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:39.720
you can use any
WSGI-compliant framework

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:41.100
that does its own routing.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:43.320
You can read more in
the documentation.

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:45.900
Also, many of App
Engine's original services

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:48.360
have matured to becoming
their own standalone Cloud

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:50.100
products, anyway,
and it's really where

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:51.630
all the new features are going.

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:54.570
So migrating lets users take
advantage of their continuing

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:56.558
innovation.

00:02:56.558 --> 00:02:57.350
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:59.690
I understand the
tech implications.

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:03.140
But before the main content,
what can you say about cost?

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:04.640
WESLEY CHUN: Yes,
it's true, Martin.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:09.230
Migration does involve regular
usage, so analyze varying costs

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:12.440
you may incur, like
datastore access, pricing

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:15.380
of the new standalone Cloud
products, free-tier quotas,

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:16.350
and so on.

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:19.070
In other words, don't go into
it blindly and get surprised

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:19.970
afterwards.

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:23.210
For this reason, the tiny sample
app used for the migrations

00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:25.070
should not incur any charges.

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:28.552
But don't let its datastore
usage go out of control.

00:03:28.552 --> 00:03:30.260
MARTIN OMANDER: Watching
videos is great,

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:32.210
but I want to get
my hands dirty.

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:33.208
Where should I go, Wes?

00:03:33.208 --> 00:03:34.500
WESLEY CHUN: All right, Martin.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:37.520
Well, except for this
video, each migration module

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:40.880
has an associated code lab,
a self-paced, free online

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:42.980
tutorial to give
you that hands-on.

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:45.470
Users get both the
starting and ending code,

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:47.150
so you can reset
back to the beginning

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:49.340
or compare your
answers with ours.

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:51.680
While some migrations
are strongly recommended,

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:52.850
others are optional.

00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:55.710
It really depends on what
makes sense for your app.

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:57.350
If you need to start
migrating today,

00:03:57.350 --> 00:03:59.430
several code labs have
already been published.

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:02.288
So there really is
no reason to wait.

00:04:02.288 --> 00:04:03.330
MARTIN OMANDER: Hey, Wes.

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:06.460
You mentioned you'll give
us a sample app to migrate.

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:08.690
Can you walk me through
that starting sample

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:11.530
so I can get an idea
of what to expect?

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:12.280
WESLEY CHUN: Sure.

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:13.570
I'm happy to, Martin.

00:04:13.570 --> 00:04:15.910
It's a simple Python
2 guestbook app

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:17.769
using the webapp2 framework.

00:04:17.769 --> 00:04:21.415
It records each page visit as
a datastore entity with the App

00:04:21.415 --> 00:04:23.163
Engine ndb client library.

00:04:23.163 --> 00:04:24.580
We're going to
dive into the code,

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:26.950
but let's pause so
you can clone the repo

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:28.680
or download the zip file.

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:29.410
OK.

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:32.080
Jump to the module 0 code,
and let's go to the computer

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:35.340
to walk through it now.

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:38.550
Let's start with the
application file main.py.

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:41.040
The key imports bring in
the webapp work framework,

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:43.920
plus its templating system,
and the App Engine ndb

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:45.930
library to access datastore.

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:47.340
Next is a data model.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:50.760
For each web page visit, we'll
autosave the timestamp as well

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:54.480
as some visitor information,
and that's it-- fairly basic.

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:57.210
store_visit creates the
entity with the visitor

00:04:57.210 --> 00:05:00.300
info we want to save, such
as IP address and browser

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:02.700
type or user agent,
then writes it

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:04.440
with a timestamp to datastore.

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:06.660
Yes, I know,
X-Forwarded-For should

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:08.050
be checked first, and so on.

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:09.960
But this is just
a sample app, so

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:12.420
grabbing the first thing
that looks like an IP address

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:15.360
rather than being
thorough and correct.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:18.000
fetch_visits queries for
the most recent visits,

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.970
which will be displayed
with the HTML template.

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:23.190
For the primary
handler, the app only

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:25.560
supports HTTP GET,
which is why it's

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:27.360
the only implemented method.

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:30.870
It registers the visit, queries
for the 10 most recent visits

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:33.420
to display, including
the one just recorded,

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:36.750
and then passes the data
to the template to render.

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:40.210
Finally, we have a routing
table at the bottom.

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:42.370
I think all "Hello
World" app.yaml's look

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:43.950
like this-- am I right?

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:49.120
It's a python27 app where all
routes go to app in main.py.

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:51.490
The last file is a
basic index template,

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:54.220
with some header info, then
a "for" loop that displays

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:55.710
the most recent visits.

00:05:55.710 --> 00:05:56.350
OK.

00:05:56.350 --> 00:05:58.690
That's a quick walk-through
of the baseline sample

00:05:58.690 --> 00:06:03.062
app you can find in the
module 0 repo folder.

00:06:03.062 --> 00:06:05.520
MARTIN OMANDER: Thanks for the
tour of the sample app, Wes.

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:07.260
Now I know what
we're starting with--

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:11.790
a basic Python 2 app using the
bundled App Engine services.

00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:15.240
By the way, is this series
for all App Engine users?

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:16.740
WESLEY CHUN: Glad
you asked, Martin.

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:18.300
Nope, it's not for everyone.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:23.430
If you're a Python 3, Java 11,
PHP 7, or Go 1.12 or newer App

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:26.460
Engine user, you're already on
the next-generation service.

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:28.170
This first set of
migration modules

00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:30.120
is only for developers,
consultants,

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:32.520
IT decision-makers,
integration specialists,

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:35.070
or any technical professional
interested in bridging

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:37.770
the gap between the first- and
second-generation App Engine

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:39.313
runtimes.

00:06:39.313 --> 00:06:40.980
MARTIN OMANDER: Thanks
for helping users

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:43.320
with these
modernization resources.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:46.373
Do you have any roadmap on
which videos we'll see first?

00:06:46.373 --> 00:06:47.790
WESLEY CHUN: Well,
thanks, Martin.

